---
import LessonLayout    from '@/layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import LessonHeader    from '@/components/LessonHeader.astro';
import LessonDiagram   from '@/components/LessonDiagram.astro';
import CodeBlock       from '@/components/CodeBlock.astro';
import LessonCallout   from '@/components/LessonCallout.astro';
import LessonChecklist from '@/components/LessonChecklist.astro';
import LessonQuiz      from '@/components/LessonQuiz.astro';
import DeepDive        from '@/components/DeepDive.astro';
import LessonMindMap   from '@/components/LessonMindMap.astro';

const reflectionCode = `
import Anthropic from '@anthropic-ai/sdk';

const client = new Anthropic();

interface ReflectionResult {
  output:       string;
  quality:      number;   // 0–10
  passed:       boolean;
  critique:     string;
  iterations:   number;
}

// The reflector is a separate model call that critiques the generator's output
async function reflect(output: string, criteria: string): Promise<{
  quality: number;
  critique: string;
  passed: boolean;
}> {
  const res = await client.messages.create({
    model: 'claude-opus-4-8',
    max_tokens: 512,
    messages: [{
      role: 'user',
      content: \`Critique the following output against these criteria:
Criteria: \${criteria}

Output to critique:
\${output}

Return JSON:
{
  "quality": <0-10>,
  "critique": "<specific improvements needed>",
  "passed": <true if quality >= 7>
}\`,
    }],
  });
  const text = res.content[0].type === 'text' ? res.content[0].text : '';
  const match = text.match(/\{[\s\S]*\}/);
  if (!match) throw new Error('Reflector did not return JSON.');
  return JSON.parse(match[0]);
}

// Generator + Reflector loop
async function generateWithReflection(
  task:       string,
  criteria:   string,
  maxLoops = 3,
): Promise<ReflectionResult> {
  let output    = '';
  let iteration = 0;

  while (iteration < maxLoops) {
    // 1. Generate (or revise based on previous critique)
    const genPrompt = iteration === 0
      ? task
      : \`Task: \${task}

Previous attempt:
\${output}

Critique:
\${lastCritique}

Please revise to address the critique.\`;

    const genRes = await client.messages.create({
      model: 'claude-opus-4-8',
      max_tokens: 1024,
      messages: [{ role: 'user', content: genPrompt }],
    });
    output = genRes.content[0].type === 'text' ? genRes.content[0].text : '';

    // 2. Reflect
    const { quality, critique, passed } = await reflect(output, criteria);
    lastCritique = critique;
    iteration++;

    if (passed) {
      return { output, quality, passed: true, critique, iterations: iteration };
    }
  }

  // Max loops reached — return best attempt
  return { output, quality: 0, passed: false, critique: lastCritique, iterations: iteration };
}

let lastCritique = '';

const result = await generateWithReflection(
  'Write a 3-sentence product description for a noise-cancelling headphone.',
  'Must mention: price range, target audience, and one key differentiator. No jargon.',
);

console.log(\`Passed after \${result.iterations} iteration(s):\`, result.passed);
console.log(result.output);
`.trim();

const mindMap = `
# Reflection Pattern
## What Is Reflection
### Generator produces draft
### Reflector critiques output
### Loop until quality passes
## Reflector Design
### Separate system prompt
### Adversarial persona
### Explicit scoring 0-10
## Reflection Variants
### Self-reflection — same model
### Cross-reflection — separate model
### Reflexion — accumulates all critiques
### Constitutional AI — fixed principles
## When to Use
### Good: writing, code, structured data
### Poor: real-time chat, simple Q&A
### Token cost doubles per loop
## Loop Control
### Max iteration cap required
### Pass threshold (e.g. score >= 7)
### Return best attempt on timeout
## Tensions
### More loops vs lower latency
### Strict threshold vs faster termination
## Pitfalls
### No maximum iteration cap
### Sycophantic reflector — same persona
### Vague criteria — no actionable direction
### Reflection on low-stakes tasks
### Not tracking iteration count
`;
---

<LessonLayout
  title="L22 — Reflection Pattern"
>
  <LessonHeader
    num="L22"
    title="Reflection Pattern"
    subtitle="A second model call critiques the generator's output and drives revision — letting the agent self-improve without human intervention."
  />

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">§3.9 — What is reflection?</h2>
    <p>
      Reflection is a loop where a <strong>generator</strong> produces an output and a
      <strong>reflector</strong> critiques it. The generator then revises based on the
      critique. This loop repeats until the output passes quality criteria or a maximum
      iteration count is reached.
    </p>

    <LessonCallout variant="key" title="Generator ≠ Reflector">
      <p>
        The most effective reflection uses a <em>separate system prompt</em> for the
        reflector — ideally a different persona or model. A reflector using the same
        system prompt as the generator tends to be sycophantic (agrees with its own output).
        Use a critical, adversarial persona for the reflector.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  T["Task"]:::input --> G["Generator\n(produce draft)"]:::gen
  G --> R{"Reflector\n(critique + score)"}:::reflect
  R -->|quality < 7| G
  R -->|quality >= 7| O["Final output"]:::output

  classDef input   fill:#f8fafc,color:#475569,stroke:#e2e8f0
  classDef gen     fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef reflect fill:#ede9fe,color:#4c1d95,stroke:#7c3aed,stroke-width:2px
  classDef output  fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
`} />

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="reflection.ts" code={reflectionCode} />

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Variants of the reflection pattern</h2>
    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Variant</th><th>Description</th><th>Use case</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Self-reflection</strong></td>
            <td>Same model critiques its own output</td>
            <td>Quick quality checks on simple outputs</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Cross-reflection</strong></td>
            <td>Separate model or system prompt critiques</td>
            <td>Higher quality, less sycophancy</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Reflexion (with memory)</strong></td>
            <td>Critique is stored; next attempt uses all past critiques</td>
            <td>Complex multi-attempt tasks like code generation</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Constitutional AI</strong></td>
            <td>Reflector uses a fixed set of principles as criteria</td>
            <td>Safety, policy compliance, brand voice</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <LessonCallout variant="warn" title="Reflection is not free">
      <p>
        Each reflection loop doubles the API calls and tokens. A 3-loop reflection
        on a 1000-token task uses 6000+ tokens total. Set a maximum loop count,
        and only use reflection for tasks where quality matters more than speed.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">When to use reflection</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Good fit:</strong> Writing tasks, code generation, structured data extraction, creative content</li>
      <li><strong>Poor fit:</strong> Real-time chat, low-latency APIs, simple Q&A, tasks where a single good prompt suffices</li>
    </ul>

    <LessonChecklist
      title="What you can now do"
      items={[
        'Implement a generate → reflect → revise loop with a max iteration cap',
        'Write a reflector prompt with explicit scoring criteria (0–10 + pass threshold)',
        'Use a separate adversarial persona for the reflector to avoid sycophancy',
        'Accumulate past critiques in the Reflexion variant for multi-attempt tasks',
        'Decide when reflection ROI justifies its token cost',
      ]}
    />

    <LessonQuiz questions={[
      {
        q:       'Why does using the same system prompt for generator and reflector reduce reflection quality?',
        options: [
          'The model cannot read its own output',
          'The reflector is sycophantic — it tends to approve outputs that match its own style',
          'Using the same system prompt causes a context length error',
          'The model confuses the generator and reflector roles',
        ],
        answer:  1,
        explain: 'A model critiquing output generated under the same system prompt has an inherent bias toward approving it — the output already matches its own style and constraints. An adversarial or neutral reflector persona catches issues the generator overlooked because it has different success criteria.',
      },
      {
        q:       'What is the primary cost of adding more reflection loops?',
        options: [
          'The generator model becomes unavailable after 3 calls',
          'Each loop doubles API calls and token usage',
          'Reflection loops break LangGraph checkpointing',
          'The model\'s context window fills up after 2 loops',
        ],
        answer:  1,
        explain: 'Each reflection iteration requires at least 2 API calls (generate + reflect). 3 loops = 6+ calls and 3x the tokens of a single generation. This is why reflection should be reserved for tasks where quality improvements justify the cost.',
      },
    ]} />

    <DeepDive
      sources="§3.9"
      tensions={[
        { a: 'More reflection loops (higher quality)', b: 'Fewer loops (lower latency & cost)' },
        { a: 'Strict pass threshold (quality gate)',   b: 'Lenient threshold (terminates faster)' },
      ]}
      questions={[
        {
          q:    'After 3 reflection loops, the output quality plateau — each revision scores 6/10 but never passes the 7/10 threshold. What is happening and how do you fix it?',
          hint: 'Think about what the generator is optimising for vs. what the reflector is asking for.',
          answer: '<p>This is a generator-reflector misalignment plateau. The generator is revising toward the critique, but the critique itself is either (1) <strong>too vague</strong> to produce actionable revisions ("improve clarity" is not actionable; "replace the passive voice in sentence 2" is), or (2) <strong>inconsistent</strong> — the reflector scores different dimensions each loop, so fixing one issue reveals another without net progress. Fixes: (1) Require the reflector to produce numbered, specific revision instructions, not general feedback. (2) Cap what the reflector can critique per loop — one dimension at a time (structure, then accuracy, then tone). (3) If quality plateaus, escape the loop early: return the best attempt and flag it for human review rather than burning tokens on no-progress iterations.</p>',
        },
        {
          q:    'How would you use the Reflexion variant (memory of all past critiques) for code generation tasks?',
          hint: 'Think about what information accumulates across attempts that would be lost with reset-per-loop.',
          answer: '<p>Standard reflection forgets previous critiques on each loop — the generator only sees the most recent feedback. For code generation, earlier failures carry useful signal: "the function throws on empty arrays" is still relevant on attempt 3 even if attempt 2\'s critique was about naming. Reflexion: (1) Accumulate all critiques in a list in state: <code>critiques: string[]</code>. (2) On each generation attempt, include the full history: "Previous attempts failed for these reasons: [list]. Ensure the new attempt addresses all of them." (3) Also accumulate the test results (failing test names, error messages) alongside prose critiques — concrete failure modes are more actionable than reflector summaries. This way, attempt 3 doesn\'t re-introduce a bug that was fixed in attempt 1.</p>',
        },
      ]}
      pitfalls={[
        'No maximum iteration cap — a stubborn generator-reflector pair can loop indefinitely.',
        'Sycophantic reflector — same persona as generator almost always passes its own output.',
        'Vague criteria — "make it better" produces inconsistent scores and no actionable revision direction.',
        'Using reflection for low-stakes tasks — 3x the cost for marginal quality gains on simple Q&A.',
        'Not tracking iteration count — no way to know if reflection actually improved quality over time.',
      ]}
    />

  </div>
  <LessonMindMap content={mindMap} />

</LessonLayout>
